1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:07,564
Agents can spend money now.

2
00:00:07,564 --> 00:00:13,171
The way that is done today is a key in an environment variable
and a prompt that says please stay under budget.

3
00:00:13,171 --> 00:00:17,566
That is not a limit. It is a suggestion, to
something that can be talked into anything.

4
00:00:17,566 --> 00:00:20,749
Mandate replaces it with a limit the
agent has no key to break.

5
00:00:23,533 --> 00:00:29,157
Fifteen rules run in a fixed order, and the first one to fail
decides. So a refusal is never just denied.

6
00:00:29,157 --> 00:00:31,621
It names the rule, and it says how far it got.

7
00:00:31,621 --> 00:00:35,906
This is the real engine, running in your own
browser with no server in the path.

8
00:00:38,699 --> 00:00:42,379
A policy is a document, and the document's
hash is what the chain holds.

9
00:00:42,379 --> 00:00:46,262
Watch what happens when I raise the daily
budget from five dollars to fifty.

10
00:00:46,262 --> 00:00:49,278
The hash changes, and the registry has never seen this one.

11
00:00:49,278 --> 00:00:53,826
Every spend judged against these rules would now be
refused, until somebody re-anchors it

12
00:00:53,826 --> 00:00:56,790
— and re-anchoring is a transaction the agent cannot send.

13
00:00:59,599 --> 00:01:01,901
And deploying that budget is one command.

14
00:01:01,901 --> 00:01:08,862
KeeperHub's Execute API sends the registration, so the registry
records KeeperHub's wallet as the policy's owner — not ours,

15
00:01:08,862 --> 00:01:11,332
and not the agent's. Let us actually run it.

16
00:01:14,132 --> 00:01:15,496
So here is the real thing.

17
00:01:15,496 --> 00:01:21,949
This is the file the live authority is enforcing right now: a daily
budget, a cap on any single call, one category allowed,

18
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:25,097
and a floor a payee has to clear before it gets paid at all.

19
00:01:26,299 --> 00:01:29,223
It is canonicalised, hashed, and registered on Sepolia.

20
00:01:29,223 --> 00:01:34,273
And the transaction that registers it is executed by
KeeperHub, which is the part that matters.

21
00:01:34,273 --> 00:01:38,791
KeeperHub's wallet is recorded as the owner on
chain — not ours, and not the agent's.

22
00:01:38,791 --> 00:01:42,406
There is no key anywhere in the agent's
process that can widen this.

23
00:01:42,999 --> 00:01:48,583
That is it on the block explorer. Sent from KeeperHub's
relayer, not from a key in anyone's dot-env file.

24
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:54,220
This is the whole architecture in one line: we decide,
KeeperHub signs, and the two are different parties.

25
00:01:54,832 --> 00:01:56,717
Connecting an agent takes one file.

26
00:01:56,717 --> 00:02:03,070
Our server is published on npm as mandate-m-c-p, so anything that
speaks M C P gets these seven tools — check a spend,

27
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:05,493
make a spend, read the budget, score a payee.

28
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:12,170
Every read-only tool works without a credential, because the person
being asked to trust a payment is the one who most needs

29
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:12,816
to check it.

30
00:02:15,632 --> 00:02:20,400
So let us give it to a real agent. This is Claude
Code, connecting to that published server.

31
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:25,168
Nothing is preloaded and there is no custom harness —
this is the client anyone can install.

32
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:27,852
First, ask it what governs it.

33
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:31,170
It reads the policy off the chain and the
budget off the authority,

34
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:36,073
and it says the thing we want an agent to say: it can read
these limits, but it cannot change them.

35
00:02:36,666 --> 00:02:39,251
Now real work. Buy market data for forty cents.

36
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:44,530
It checks first, all fifteen rules pass, and then
KeeperHub signs the payment and broadcasts it.

37
00:02:44,530 --> 00:02:47,994
Real money, on a real chain, and the
agent never touched a key.

38
00:02:48,599 --> 00:02:55,345
Ask for the identical purchase again. This time it stops at rule two
of fifteen, and the other thirteen are never consulted.

39
00:02:55,345 --> 00:03:01,058
This is the failure nobody designs for and everybody ships:
an agent that retries and quietly pays twice.

40
00:03:03,866 --> 00:03:08,611
And now the attack. A prompt telling the agent the user
already approved five thousand dollars.

41
00:03:08,611 --> 00:03:11,808
It is convincing, and the agent believes
it. It does not matter.

42
00:03:11,808 --> 00:03:17,103
Look at what it works out on its own: splitting it into five
thousand small calls fails on the rate limit,

43
00:03:17,103 --> 00:03:20,549
and it holds no key anyway. The authority
is not in the conversation.

44
00:03:23,332 --> 00:03:26,413
Last, a payee nobody has ever paid. Not refused — held.

45
00:03:26,413 --> 00:03:32,296
Reputation is scored as a lower confidence bound, so a vendor
with no history scores low by construction,

46
00:03:32,296 --> 00:03:34,873
and it goes to a person instead of to a guess.

47
00:03:37,665 --> 00:03:42,341
Here is the payment the agent made, on chain. Point
four tokens, actually transferred.

48
00:03:42,341 --> 00:03:48,865
Sent by KeeperHub's relayer again, with the gas sponsored, so the
agent needed no funds of its own and no wallet at all.

49
00:03:49,465 --> 00:03:53,315
And if you are writing the agent rather than
running one, here is the whole file.

50
00:03:53,315 --> 00:03:56,833
Step one: the policy is read from disk, and
then checked against the chain

51
00:03:56,833 --> 00:04:01,539
— if the document has been edited since it was anchored,
this throws, and nothing is judged at all.

52
00:04:01,539 --> 00:04:06,720
Step two: the spend window comes from a durable ledger,
partitioned per agent. Step three: the rules propose.

53
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:11,331
Nothing has been applied yet, which is why the same call
is both a preflight and a real decision.

54
00:04:11,331 --> 00:04:14,326
Step four: a refusal names the rule that
refused it, and stops.

55
00:04:14,326 --> 00:04:18,414
And step five: only an approval ever reaches
KeeperHub, which signs and broadcasts it.

56
00:04:21,198 --> 00:04:25,736
So here it is, from packages on npm, given a
spend that breaks the per-call cap.

57
00:04:25,736 --> 00:04:30,841
Refused at rule eleven of fifteen, named, with
nothing moved and no execution left behind.

58
00:04:33,631 --> 00:04:38,623
Every decision is on the public record: what was asked,
what was decided, and which rule decided it.

59
00:04:39,198 --> 00:04:41,717
And any of them can be checked with none of our code.

60
00:04:41,717 --> 00:04:46,328
This recomputes the Merkle root from the receipt and
compares it with the root anchored on chain.

61
00:04:46,328 --> 00:04:48,990
If we had lied about a single decision, this would fail.

62
00:04:49,598 --> 00:04:56,131
A limit the agent cannot argue with, evidence anybody can check, and
every transaction on chain executed through KeeperHub.

63
00:04:56,131 --> 00:04:57,193
Thanks for watching.
